-A rasterization technique where each voxel in a cluster is processed by a single invocation, projecting its footprint box to the pixel centers it covers, replacing the previous method of walking every pixel of a brick's rectangle through its cells.
+A rasterization technique where each voxel in a cluster is processed by a single invocation, projecting its footprint box to the pixel centers it covers, replacing the previous method of walking every pixel of a brick's rectangle through its cells. It solves the inefficiency of processing pixels that no voxel footprint covers, particularly at shallow voxel levels where footprints are small fractions of cell sizes.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of leaf voxels, $O(N)$, as each voxel generates a constant number of vertices (4) and triangles (2).
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low overhead. Only leaf voxel data needs to be stored and transferred. The geometry shader generates geometry on the fly, avoiding large intermediate buffers [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Efficient on hardware with capable geometry shaders. Performance scales with the number of visible leaf voxels. Culling (frustum and face) significantly reduces the number of splats processed [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation; leverages standard graphics pipeline; allows for easy integration of lighting (via normals) and transparency (via blending) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Does not handle occlusion efficiently (all splats are rendered, though back-facing ones are culled); no depth sorting is performed, which can cause artifacts with transparent voxels unless sorted or using alpha-to-coverage [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time visualization of sparse voxel data where simplicity and speed are prioritized over perfect occlusion handling.
-*   **When not to use:** Scenes requiring high-quality transparency with complex occlusion, or when the number of voxels is extremely large and geometry shader overhead becomes a bottleneck.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of leaf voxels $N_{leaves}$. Each leaf is processed in constant time by the geometry shader.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Low. Only leaf voxel data needs to be stored in vertex buffers. The octree structure itself is stored in memory but not directly rendered.
+*   **Performance:** Efficient for sparse datasets where the number of visible leaves is small compared to the total volume. Performance degrades if the scene is dense and nearly all leaves are visible, as the geometry shader overhead becomes significant.
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation using standard OpenGL geometry shaders. Effective culling reduces overdraw.
+*   **Limitations:** Geometry shader overhead can be high on some hardware. Not suitable for very dense volumes without additional optimization (like the child method). Transparency handling requires sorting or alpha blending, which is not detailed in the source.
 
